<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#sakdina">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sakdina</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sakdina was a system of social hierarchy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> to the early Rattanakosin period, assigning each person a numerical rank by status that set their precedence in society, above all among the nobility. The numbers stood for the rai of land a person was entitled to own, the word meaning 'field prestige', though there is no evidence that it was applied literally: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-seals-law">Three Seals Law</a> gives 100,000 for the Maha Uparat, 600 for learned monks, 20 for commoners and 5 for slaves. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borommatrailokkanat">Borommatrailokkanat</a> fixed the scale by law in 1454. Since 1945 the word has served as a critique of Thai political authority, and protesters in 2020–2021 used it against authoritarian values.</p></div>
